---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/home/Hero.astro';
import HomeSection from '../components/home/HomeSection.astro';
import VerdictAnatomy from '../components/home/VerdictAnatomy.astro';
import SameReadout from '../components/home/SameReadout.astro';
import PlanTable from '../components/home/PlanTable.astro';
import DecisionFigure from '../components/home/DecisionFigure.astro';
import FourSteps from '../components/home/FourSteps.astro';
import CodeChecks from '../components/home/CodeChecks.astro';
import Stack from '../components/home/Stack.astro';
import Limits from '../components/home/Limits.astro';
import TryIt from '../components/home/TryIt.astro';
import SystematicsMatrix from '../components/sensing/SystematicsMatrix.astro';
import { RUN } from '../data/crosstalk';
import { GRAVIMETER } from '../data/sensing';
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'DeepCausality Quantum | Find the cause of a quantum error';
const description =
  'A Rust library that finds the causal structure behind correlated qubit errors and the systematic behind a sensor’s offset, verifies error-correcting codes exactly without simulating them, and bounds the error of stacked codes. Every verdict reports what it examined, its margin, and whether an exact or a numeric path decided it.';
const seconds = (s: number) => s.toLocaleString('en-US');

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'deep_causality_quantum',
  description,
  programmingLanguage: 'Rust',
  codeRepository: GITHUB_URL,
  license: 'https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT',
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path="/">
  <SiteHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero />

  <HomeSection ix="02 / 10" label="What a verdict carries" title="Every verdict shows its evidence.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        A bare pass looks the same as a check that examined nothing. Every check in the library
        returns one record with the four properties below.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <VerdictAnatomy />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="03 / 10" label="Which cause?" title="Passive data cannot separate these causes.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Two qubits show correlated errors. Qubit 1 may drive qubit 2, qubit 2 may drive qubit 1,
        or a shared bath may drive both. The three structures are Markov equivalent: each fits
        the passive observation exactly, so more passive data separates nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        An intervention separates them. Hold qubit 1 excited and read qubit 2; the number
        returned is the read-out. H1 predicts 0.40, and H2 and H3 each predict 0.10. You supply
        each candidate’s conditional tables and a response model for each experiment; the library
        computes the predictions and plans from them.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <SameReadout />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="04 / 10" label="The plan and the decision" title="Two experiments separate all three causes.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        The planner picks E1 and E2 at a total cost of {RUN.planCost}, of the
        {RUN.allExperimentsCost} that all four experiments cost in the example’s arbitrary unit.
        Its minimum-cost cover requires a chosen experiment to separate each pair of candidates by
        at least {RUN.floorBits} bits at that experiment’s shot count, and it lists any pair that
        no experiment reaches.
      </p>
      <p>
        E1 (hold qubit 1 excited) separates H1 from the others, and E2 (hold qubit 2 excited)
        separates H2 from the others. At cost 1 each, together they cover all three pairs. The
        passive run E0 separates nothing.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <PlanTable />
    <div class="decision">
      <h3>The decision</h3>
      <p>
        A campaign runs the plan’s cheapest experiment, E1, on data the simulator draws from H1’s
        prediction, and judges each candidate against it. One survives, separated from both
        rivals, so the campaign stops after one experiment and spends {RUN.campaignSpent} of the
        plan’s {RUN.planCost}.
      </p>
      <DecisionFigure />
    </div>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="05 / 10" label="The program" title="One Rust program declares, screens, plans and decides.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        The crosstalk program declares the problem, screens the candidates, plans the
        experiments and names a survivor. Each card shows the call for its step.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <FourSteps />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="06 / 10" label="Which systematic?" title="Find the cause of a gravimeter’s offset without the 5.6-hour scan.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        After the tide correction, a cold-atom gravimeter reads 5 µGal low, and seven systematics
        can each produce that offset. The planner prices each experiment as setup plus
        integration time and picks six that separate every pair, for
        {seconds(GRAVIMETER.planCost)} s.
      </p>
      <p>
        Those six already separate the wavefront from each rival, so the plan skips its
        {seconds(20000)} s temperature scan and identifies the wavefront by elimination. A campaign
        finds each of the seven causes in {GRAVIMETER.cheapest} s to
        {seconds(GRAVIMETER.dearest)} s. The example simulates every observation, and its setup
        times and tide stand in for a lab’s values.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <SystematicsMatrix />
    <p class="sensing-more">
      <a href="/sensing/">The sensing section: the gradiometer, and eight published results rerun <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
    </p>
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="07 / 10" label="Is the code sound?" title="Four exact checks verify a code.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        A CSS code puts qubits on a lattice’s edges, Z checks on its faces and X checks on its
        vertices. QCL reads the lattice as a chain complex and decides four questions from
        supports over 𝔽₂, with exact rational phases. It builds no state vector, so it reaches
        the 32-qubit [[32, 2]] toric code although the crate’s simulator stops at 24 qubits.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <CodeChecks />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="08 / 10" label="What do stacked codes cost?" title="The composition law bounds the error of stacked codes.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        An abstraction says a simple model summarises a detailed one, as two logical qubits stand
        for eight physical ones. QCL measures the residual, the distance between two channels:
        the detailed circuit followed by the ideal recovery, and the simple circuit. When
        abstractions stack, the law <code>ε ≤ ‖τ₂‖_post · ε₁ + ‖τ₁‖_pre · ε₂</code> bounds the
        composite residual by the links’ residuals (Lorenz and Tull, arXiv:2602.16612).
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <Stack />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection ix="09 / 10" label="Limits" title="Where the library stops.">
    <Limits />
  </HomeSection>

  <HomeSection id="run" ix="10 / 10" label="Run it" title="One command reruns the example.">
    <Fragment slot="lede">
      <p>
        Three shell lines clone the repository and run the crosstalk example. The other examples
        run the same way.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
    <TryIt />
  </HomeSection>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .decision { margin-top: var(--space-7); }
  .decision h3 { font-size: var(--t-h3); margin: 0 0 var(--space-2); }
  .decision > p { color: var(--fg-1); max-width: var(--measure); margin: 0 0 var(--space-5); }
  .sensing-more { margin: var(--space-5) 0 0; font-size: var(--t-body-sm); }
</style>
